1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,457
Your bank S.M.S. says eight hundred and forty rupees — debited.

2
00:00:04,457 --> 00:00:07,094
Your U.P.I. app says — processing.

3
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:08,713
And the shop's soundbox?

4
00:00:08,713 --> 00:00:09,806
Silent.

5
00:00:09,806 --> 00:00:11,896
The shopkeeper is looking at you.

6
00:00:11,896 --> 00:00:13,301
Don't pay again.

7
00:00:13,301 --> 00:00:15,024
Your money is not lost.

8
00:00:15,024 --> 00:00:22,083
In a few minutes, you'll know exactly where it is, when it comes back, and the one mistake that makes people pay twice.

9
00:00:22,083 --> 00:00:24,590
Here's what happens after you type your pin.

10
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:27,650
First, your bank takes the money out of your account.

11
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:29,639
That's the debited message you see.

12
00:00:29,639 --> 00:00:31,767
Then N.P.C.I. steps in.

13
00:00:31,767 --> 00:00:35,831
It's not your app — it's the system behind all U.P.I. apps.

14
00:00:35,831 --> 00:00:40,419
It calls the shop's bank and says, please add this money to the shop's account.

15
00:00:40,419 --> 00:00:42,547
I like to think of it as a phone call.

16
00:00:42,547 --> 00:00:46,097
And like every phone call in India... three things can happen.

17
00:00:46,097 --> 00:00:51,792
One, the shop's bank picks up and says, done, the shop got it — that's success.

18
00:00:51,792 --> 00:00:59,566
Two, the shop's bank picks up and says, sorry, we can't take this, maybe there's a problem with the account — that's failed.

19
00:00:59,566 --> 00:01:02,850
Failed sounds scary, but it's the clear answer.

20
00:01:02,850 --> 00:01:07,186
The payment did not go through, and your money comes back to you on its own.

21
00:01:07,186 --> 00:01:09,832
So if it says failed, you can pay again.

22
00:01:09,832 --> 00:01:11,114
Simple so far, right?

23
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:11,828
Three.

24
00:01:11,828 --> 00:01:13,326
The troublemaker.

25
00:01:13,326 --> 00:01:16,953
N.P.C.I. calls... and the line goes quiet.

26
00:01:16,953 --> 00:01:17,918
Hello?...

27
00:01:17,918 --> 00:01:18,897
Hello?...

28
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:20,047
Call dropped.

29
00:01:20,047 --> 00:01:23,272
Did the shop's bank add the money before the call dropped?

30
00:01:23,272 --> 00:01:24,541
Nobody knows yet.

31
00:01:24,541 --> 00:01:28,103
That's the processing you see — some apps call it pending.

32
00:01:28,103 --> 00:01:30,929
So right there at the counter, don't pay again.

33
00:01:30,929 --> 00:01:34,993
Ask the shopkeeper to check their bank S.M.S., not the soundbox.

34
00:01:34,993 --> 00:01:38,434
And take a screenshot of your payment, so you both have proof.

35
00:01:38,434 --> 00:01:39,792
Bank says received?

36
00:01:39,792 --> 00:01:42,152
Then the shop got your money, and you're done.

37
00:01:42,152 --> 00:01:43,088
Nothing yet?

38
00:01:43,088 --> 00:01:44,839
Then it's still processing.

39
00:01:44,839 --> 00:01:48,616
Show them your screenshot and say — it's stuck, not lost.

40
00:01:48,616 --> 00:01:53,042
If it reaches you, you'll get the S.M.S. If not, it comes back to me.

41
00:01:53,042 --> 00:01:55,234
So where is your money right now?

42
00:01:55,234 --> 00:02:00,274
When the call drops, N.P.C.I. doesn't cancel the payment — it puts it on hold.

43
00:02:00,274 --> 00:02:05,216
Your money has left your account, but it's stuck between your bank and the shop's bank.

44
00:02:05,216 --> 00:02:07,193
Not lost — just waiting.

45
00:02:07,193 --> 00:02:13,126
Now the shop's bank has to answer one simple question — did it go into the shop's account, or not?

46
00:02:13,126 --> 00:02:14,534
Only two answers.

47
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:15,209
Yes?

48
00:02:15,209 --> 00:02:18,845
Then the shop has your money, even though the soundbox stayed silent.

49
00:02:18,845 --> 00:02:19,513
No?

50
00:02:19,513 --> 00:02:22,753
Then it comes back to your bank, and into your account.

51
00:02:22,753 --> 00:02:24,328
How long can this take?

52
00:02:24,328 --> 00:02:32,285
If the money didn't reach them — whether it says failed or processing — the bank must return it, and the R.B.I. has set a deadline.

53
00:02:32,285 --> 00:02:33,327
Paid a person?

54
00:02:33,327 --> 00:02:34,690
Back by the next day.

55
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:36,259
Paid a shop or a business?

56
00:02:36,259 --> 00:02:41,958
Within five days — five normal days, Sundays included, not five working days.

57
00:02:41,958 --> 00:02:43,389
Person, one day.

58
00:02:43,389 --> 00:02:44,913
Shop, five days.

59
00:02:44,913 --> 00:02:50,550
And if the bank takes longer, it has to pay you one hundred rupees for every extra day.

60
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:52,044
You don't even have to complain.

61
00:02:52,044 --> 00:02:54,298
The bank has to pay it on its own.

62
00:02:54,298 --> 00:02:55,881
That's an R.B.I. rule.

63
00:02:55,881 --> 00:02:58,317
Quick summary, the way I remember it.

64
00:02:58,317 --> 00:03:00,569
Success — the shop got it.

65
00:03:00,569 --> 00:03:05,558
Failed — it didn't go through, your money comes back on its own, and you can pay again.

66
00:03:05,558 --> 00:03:07,720
Processing — don't pay again.

67
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:13,515
If it never reaches the shop, you get it back within one day for a person, five days for a shop.

68
00:03:13,515 --> 00:03:17,311
My one rule: never pay again while it says processing.

69
00:03:17,311 --> 00:03:24,792
Because if that first payment turns out to be a yes... congrats, you just paid eight hundred and forty rupees... twice.

70
00:03:24,792 --> 00:03:25,877
Don't want to wait?

71
00:03:25,877 --> 00:03:31,366
Open your app, go to your payment history, tap that payment, and look for U.P.I. Help.

72
00:03:31,366 --> 00:03:33,024
You can raise it right away.

73
00:03:33,024 --> 00:03:34,515
Paid twice by mistake?

74
00:03:34,515 --> 00:03:36,362
Choose duplicate payment there.

75
00:03:36,362 --> 00:03:37,601
Still not solved?

76
00:03:37,601 --> 00:03:39,081
Complain to your bank.

77
00:03:39,081 --> 00:03:40,353
Bank not fixing it?

78
00:03:40,353 --> 00:03:45,939
Then complain to the R.B.I. The office has a strange name — the R.B.I. Ombudsman.

79
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:46,844
Remember it:

80
00:03:46,844 --> 00:03:48,621
R.B.I. Ombudsman.

81
00:03:48,621 --> 00:03:53,220
Send this to that one friend who always pays twice — I know you have one.

82
00:03:53,220 --> 00:03:59,386
Next video: what really happens in those few seconds after you hit pay, when everything works.

83
00:03:59,386 --> 00:04:00,509
Watch that one next.

